{
  "id": "js-101",
  "name": "JavaScript Introduction",
  "description": "Introduction to JavaScript in Arima Notebooks — modern JS syntax, arrays, objects, and async patterns",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "js-intro-md-1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# JavaScript in Arima Notebooks\n\nArima supports **JavaScript** (Node.js) alongside JShell and Java.\n\n- Click the mode button on any code cell to cycle: **JShell → Java → JS**\n- JavaScript cells run via Node.js — each cell is independent\n- Use `require('package')` to import npm packages\n- Built-in `barista` helpers: `barista.table()`, `barista.display()`, `barista.stats()`\n\n> **Install npm packages** in the Packages tab → npm section.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: hello-world-in-java-script\n//@ description: Hello World in JavaScript\n// Hello World in JavaScript\n\n// Each JS cell runs as its own fresh Node.js process. Nothing carries over from\n// the cell above, so every cell must declare whatever it needs for itself.\n\n// console.log is Node's standard output. Given several arguments it prints them\n// separated by a single space, so you rarely need to concatenate strings by hand.\nconsole.log('Hello from Arima Notebooks!');\n\n// `process` is a global object that Node injects into every script. It describes\n// the running process rather than the language itself, which is why it exists in\n// Node but not in a browser.\nconsole.log('Node.js version:', process.version);   // e.g. \"v22.11.0\"\nconsole.log('Platform:', process.platform);         // \"win32\" | \"darwin\" | \"linux\"",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "hello-world-in-java-script",
      "description": "Hello World in JavaScript"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-md-2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Modern JavaScript Syntax\n\nArima runs Node.js, so all ES2022+ features are available.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: arrow-functions-destructuring-spread\n//@ description: Arrow functions, destructuring, spread\n// Arrow functions, destructuring, spread\n\n// `const` freezes the BINDING, not the contents. `numbers` can never point at a\n// different array, but the array itself is still mutable. Prefer const by default\n// and reach for `let` only where you genuinely reassign.\nconst numbers = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3];\n\n// Array methods\n// A Set keeps only the first occurrence of each value, so it is the idiomatic way\n// to deduplicate. `...` (spread) then pours the Set back into a plain array,\n// because a Set has no .sort() of its own.\n// The comparator matters: sort() with no arguments compares values as STRINGS,\n// which would put 10 before 2. `(a, b) => a - b` gives numeric order. Note that\n// sort() mutates in place - here it mutates the fresh array from the spread,\n// never `numbers`.\nconst unique  = [...new Set(numbers)].sort((a,b) => a-b);\n\n// filter keeps the elements for which the callback returns something truthy.\n// `===` is strict equality: no type coercion. `0 == '0'` is true but\n// `0 === '0'` is false, which is why `===` is the default choice in modern JS.\nconst evens   = numbers.filter(n => n % 2 === 0);\n\n// map returns a NEW array of the same length, one output per input. The original\n// array is untouched - that is what makes these methods safe to chain.\nconst doubled = numbers.map(n => n * 2);\n\n// reduce folds an array down to one value. The callback receives the accumulator\n// so far and the current element, and returns the next accumulator. The trailing\n// `0` is the seed; supplying it also makes reduce safe on an empty array, which\n// would otherwise throw.\nconst sum     = numbers.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);\n\n// join() renders an array as a single string with the given separator.\nconsole.log('Original:', numbers.join(', '));\nconsole.log('Unique sorted:', unique.join(', '));\nconsole.log('Evens:', evens.join(', '));\nconsole.log('Doubled:', doubled.join(', '));\n// toFixed(2) rounds to two decimal places and returns a STRING, not a number.\nconsole.log('Sum:', sum, '  Average:', (sum / numbers.length).toFixed(2));",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "arrow-functions-destructuring-spread",
      "description": "Arrow functions, destructuring, spread"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: objects-classes-and-destructuring\n//@ description: Objects, classes, and destructuring\n// Objects, classes, and destructuring\n\n// `class` is syntax over JavaScript's prototype system - readable OOP notation\n// on top of the same underlying machinery.\nclass Person {\n  // The constructor runs when you write `new Person(...)`. `this` refers to the\n  // object being built; assigning to `this.name` creates the property.\n  constructor(name, age, role) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.age  = age;\n    this.role = role;\n  }\n  // Overriding toString() controls how the object renders when JavaScript needs\n  // a string from it, for example in string concatenation.\n  toString() { return `${this.name} (${this.age}) — ${this.role}`; }\n}\n\n// `new` allocates a fresh object, wires up the prototype, and runs the constructor.\nconst team = [\n  new Person('Alice',  30, 'Engineer'),\n  new Person('Bob',    25, 'Designer'),\n  new Person('Carol',  35, 'Manager'),\n  new Person('Dave',   28, 'Engineer'),\n];\n\n// Display as table using Arima helper\n// barista.table wants plain data objects, so map each Person into a small record\n// whose keys become the column headers.\nbarista.table(team.map(p => ({ Name: p.name, Age: p.age, Role: p.role })));\n\n// Destructuring\n// Object destructuring pulls properties out by NAME, so the variable names on\n// the left must match the property names. Order is irrelevant, unlike arrays.\nconst { name, age } = team[0];\n// `\\n` inside a template literal is a real newline; template literals also let\n// you embed expressions with ${...} instead of gluing strings together with +.\nconsole.log(`\\nFirst team member: ${name}, age ${age}`);",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "objects-classes-and-destructuring",
      "description": "Objects, classes, and destructuring"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-md-3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Working with Data\n\nJSON manipulation and functional programming patterns.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: functional-data-processing\n//@ description: Functional data processing\n// Functional data processing\n\n// An array of plain objects is JavaScript's everyday \"table of rows\".\nconst sales = [\n  { month: 'Jan', region: 'North', amount: 12400 },\n  { month: 'Jan', region: 'South', amount:  8900 },\n  { month: 'Feb', region: 'North', amount: 15600 },\n  { month: 'Feb', region: 'South', amount: 11200 },\n  { month: 'Mar', region: 'North', amount: 13800 },\n  { month: 'Mar', region: 'South', amount: 14300 },\n];\n\n// Group by region\n// reduce is not only for numbers - the accumulator can be any shape. Here it is\n// an object used as a lookup table, built up one row at a time.\nconst byRegion = sales.reduce((acc, row) => {\n  // `acc[row.region] || []` supplies an empty array the first time a region is\n  // seen. The assignment is nested inside the parentheses so that .push() runs\n  // against whichever array ends up stored - a compact \"get or create\" idiom.\n  (acc[row.region] = acc[row.region] || []).push(row.amount);\n  // reduce requires you to RETURN the accumulator each pass; forgetting this is\n  // the single most common reduce bug.\n  return acc;\n}, {});   // {} is the seed: start from an empty object\n\nconsole.log('Sales by region:');\n// Object.entries turns { North: [...], South: [...] } into [['North', [...]], ...].\n// The `([region, amounts])` parameter destructures each of those pairs in place.\nObject.entries(byRegion).forEach(([region, amounts]) => {\n  const total = amounts.reduce((s,a) => s+a, 0);\n  // toFixed(0) rounds to a whole number and returns a string.\n  const avg   = (total / amounts.length).toFixed(0);\n  // toLocaleString adds the reader's local thousands separators, e.g. \"28,000\".\n  console.log(`  ${region}: total=${total.toLocaleString()}  avg=${avg}`);\n});\n\nconsole.log('\\nFull dataset:');\nbarista.table(sales);",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "functional-data-processing",
      "description": "Functional data processing"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: async-await-with-promises\n//@ description: Async/await with Promises\n// Async/await with Promises\n\n// A Promise is an object standing in for a value that is not ready yet. Wrapping\n// setTimeout in one converts a callback-style API into something awaitable.\nasync function delay(ms) {\n  return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\n}\n\n// `async` marks a function whose return value is automatically wrapped in a\n// Promise, and it is the ONLY place `await` is allowed. That restriction is what\n// keeps the surrounding code from blocking: await suspends just this function\n// and hands control back to the event loop until the Promise settles.\nasync function fetchSimulated(id) {\n  await delay(10); // simulate network\n  return { id, data: `Record ${id}`, timestamp: new Date().toISOString() };\n}\n\nasync function main() {\n  console.log('Fetching records in parallel...');\n  // .map(...) starts all five calls immediately and collects the pending\n  // Promises. Promise.all then waits for the whole set, so the total wait is\n  // about one delay rather than five. Awaiting inside a loop instead would\n  // serialise them - a classic and easily missed performance mistake.\n  const results = await Promise.all([1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(id => fetchSimulated(id)));\n  barista.table(results.map(r => ({ ID: r.id, Data: r.data })));\n  console.log('All done!');\n}\n\n// Calling main() returns a Promise. Nothing awaits it here, so this line simply\n// schedules the work; Node keeps the process alive until the timers have fired.\nmain();",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "async-await-with-promises",
      "description": "Async/await with Promises"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-6",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: template-literals-optional-chaining-nu\n//@ description: Template literals, optional chaining, nullish coalescing\n// Template literals, optional chaining, nullish coalescing\n\nconst config = {\n  server: { host: 'localhost', port: 8585 },\n  db: null,\n};\n\n// `?.` is optional chaining: if the value to its left is null or undefined the\n// whole expression short-circuits to undefined instead of throwing\n// \"Cannot read properties of null\".\n// `??` is nullish coalescing: it supplies the right-hand side only when the left\n// is null or undefined. This differs from `||`, which also fires for 0, '' and\n// false - so `??` is the correct choice whenever 0 or '' is a valid value.\nconst host    = config.server?.host ?? 'unknown';\nconst dbHost  = config.db?.host     ?? 'not configured';   // config.db is null\nconst timeout = config.server?.timeout ?? 30;              // property is absent\n\nconsole.log(`Server: ${host}:${config.server.port}`);\nconsole.log(`Database host: ${dbHost}`);\nconsole.log(`Timeout: ${timeout}s`);\n\n// Tagged template literal\n// Prefixing a template literal with a function name calls that function instead\n// of building a string directly. It receives the literal text chunks as an array\n// and the interpolated values as the rest arguments, so it can transform them -\n// this is how libraries implement escaping, i18n and syntax highlighting.\nfunction highlight(strings, ...values) {\n  // There is always one more text chunk than value, hence the undefined check.\n  return strings.reduce((acc, str, i) => acc + str + (values[i] !== undefined ? `[${values[i]}]` : ''), '');\n}\nconsole.log(highlight`Arima runs on port ${8585} using Node ${process.version}`);",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "template-literals-optional-chaining-nu",
      "description": "Template literals, optional chaining, nullish coalescing"
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-md-4",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Arima Built-in Helpers\n\nEvery JS cell has access to a `barista` object with display utilities:\n\n| Helper | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| `barista.table(arrayOfObjects)` | Print a formatted text table |\n| `barista.display(anything)` | Pretty-print JSON |\n| `barista.stats(numberArray)` | count, min, max, mean, std dev |",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "js-intro-7",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "nodejs",
      "source": "//@ anchor: barista-built-in-helpers-demo\n//@ description: Arima built-in helpers demo\n// Arima built-in helpers demo\n\n// `barista` is injected into every JS cell by Arima - it is not part of Node.\nconst data = [\n  { name: 'mercury', distanceAU: 0.39, moons: 0 },\n  { name: 'barista',   distanceAU: 0.72, moons: 0 },\n  { name: 'earth',   distanceAU: 1.00, moons: 1 },\n  { name: 'mars',    distanceAU: 1.52, moons: 2 },\n  { name: 'jupiter', distanceAU: 5.20, moons: 95 },\n];\n\n// table() takes an array of objects with matching keys; the keys become columns.\nbarista.table(data);\n\nconsole.log('\\nDistance stats (AU):');\n// stats() expects a flat array of numbers, so project out the column first.\nbarista.stats(data.map(p => p.distanceAU));\n\nconsole.log('\\nJSON display:');\n// display() pretty-prints any value as JSON - handy for nested objects that\n// console.log would otherwise abbreviate as [Object].\nbarista.display({ planet: 'Arima', symbol: '♀', type: 'Terrestrial' });",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "barista-built-in-helpers-demo",
      "description": "Arima built-in helpers demo"
    }
  ],
